\title{Reed–Solomon and Linear Codes}
\author{dhsorens}
\date{2026-08-18}

\p{
    Proof systems in this family work because a low-degree polynomial is a codeword and a cheating
    prover is not. The coding theory is therefore not background — it is where soundness comes from.
}

\p{
    Questions this page should answer:
    \ul{
        \li{Reed–Solomon codes: rate, distance, the Singleton bound.}
        \li{Rate as the central dial — proof size against prover time — and where deployed systems
        set it.}
        \li{General linear codes and tensor codes; the Ligero and Brakedown line as an alternative
        to RS.}
        \li{Interleaved codes, and what proximity to a code means for a matrix of columns rather
        than a single word.}
        \li{Foldable and linear-time-encodable codes, and which protocols need which property.}
    }
}

\p{
    Sources: [SoK: Hash-Based Polynomial Commitments and Low-Degree
    Tests](https://eprint.iacr.org/2026/1367).
}
